AB  - AI agents represent a significant shift in how software systems use large language models (LLMs), with a new class of agents that reason, plan and call external tools as well as services. The communication layer between an agent and its tools has emerged as the binding constraint on security, reliability along with interoperability of agent–based systems, yet the protocol layer remains thin in existing surveys. Our study aim is to understand and map the research topics, challenges and future directions concerning agent–tool communication. A systematic mapping study (SMS) is therefore implemented to classify the selected papers into four (04) thematic categories. Categories cover protocol design, security and authentication frameworks, tool discovery alongside orchestration and interoperability, with production deployment and migration as the fourth. We have selected 112 academic primary studies plus 13 industrial reports across five (05) phases of selection and analysis, drawn from the search window 2020 to 2026. Cross-analyses surface comparisons across open agent–tool communication contracts namely MCP, A2A, ACP and ANP alongside the LangChain and AutoGPT framework family on authentication, authorization, input validation and sandboxing posture, with security threats paired against mitigation strategies reported in the same studies. From the findings we have distilled five (05) research gaps. Gaps cover protocol standardisation, security-by-design, orchestration formalisation, cross-framework interoperability and lifecycle governance, paired with future directions, two of them carried through to a concrete first step, which may help researchers and practitioners design safer agent–based systems.
ER  - 

