AB  - Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in a wide range of vision-language tasks. However, the large number of visual tokens introduces significant computational overhead. To address this issue, visual token pruning has emerged as a key technique for enhancing the efficiency of MLLMs. In cognitive science, humans tend to first determine which regions of a scene to attend to (“where to look”) before deciding which specific elements within those regions to process in detail (“what to select”). This sequential process supports efficient use of limited attention: it first allocates attention to candidate regions and then refines processing within the selected regions. However, existing pruning methods primarily focus on directly optimizing “what to select”, typically using attention scores or similarity metrics. They rarely consider “where to look”, which has been shown to lead to inefficient spatial allocation, positional bias, and the retention of irrelevant or redundant tokens. In this paper, we propose GridPrune, a method that replaces the global Top-K mechanism with a “guide-globally, select-locally” zonal selection system. GridPrune splits the pruning process into two steps: first, it uses text-conditional guidance to dynamically allocate a token budget across spatial zones; then, it performs local selection within each budgeted zone. Experimental results demonstrate that GridPrune achieves superior performance across various MLLM architectures. On LLaVA-NeXT-7B, GridPrune retains 96.98% of the full performance while using 11.1% of the tokens, outperforming the best-performing baseline by 2.34% at the same pruning rate. The code is available at https://github.com/NONO997/GridPrune.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - Semanti-MOP: Bridging the semantic gap in automated architecture refactoring via instability-aware Large Language Models
AU  - Salem, Ahmed
JO  - Information and Software Technology
VL  - 199
SP  - 108280
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/11/01/
SN  - 0950-5849
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2026.108280
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584926002697
KW  - Microservice decomposition
KW  - Search-Based Software Engineering
KW  - Many-objective optimization
KW  - Large Language Models
KW  - Software refactoring
KW  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
AB  - Context:
The architectural decomposition of monolithic software into microservices is an NP-hard combinatorial problem often hampered by the Semantic–Structural Conflict. This conflict arises when structural metrics (such as coupling and cohesion) reach a plateau of Pareto-optimal solutions that are technically indistinguishable yet correspond to semantically divergent service boundaries, often leading to the violation of domain intent. Conversely, Large Language Models (LLMs) possess deep semantic intuition, but their application as global classifiers is computationally prohibitive and unconstrained by explicit structural objective functions.
Objectives:
This paper introduces Semanti-MOP, a novel instability-triggered control mechanism for integrating LLMs into SBSE that harmonizes many-objective evolutionary optimization with targeted LLM-driven semantic steering to resolve conflicts between technical modularity and domain semantic intent.
Methods:
The framework incorporates a structural instability-triggered repair mechanism that monitors the Pareto-optimal population for service assignment entropy. It selectively invokes an LLM via Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) only when structural metrics yield high uncertainty (Φ), allowing the LLM to act as a semantic arbiter to resolve points of structural symmetry. The LLM acts as a semantic arbiter through a memetic repair operator to steer the search toward logically valid boundaries. We evaluate Semanti-MOP on three industrial-scale target projects — Spring-PetClinic, Cargo Tracker, and Shopizer — across seven state-of-the-art optimizers totaling 2520 independent runs for the primary evaluation.To further assess scalability and robustness on a truly massive codebase, an additional 840 independent runs were conducted on Apache Flink, a massive industrial system, across two scenarios.
Results:
Results from the main evaluation show that Semanti-MOP statistically outperforms purely structural baselines, achieving significant semantic accuracy improvements (MoJoFM) with large effect sizes (yielding A12 up to 0.86) while simultaneously improving search quality (Hypervolume). Real-time delta plots confirm that LLM steering provides instantaneous corrective spikes, overcoming misleading “structural pull.” The dedicated experiments on Apache Flink further demonstrate Semanti-MOP’s robust performance and efficient LLM utilization on an extremely large system, confirming its scalability for industrial application.
Conclusion:
The selective trigger mechanism significantly minimizes computational overhead, demonstrating that Semanti-MOP is a scalable, cost-efficient, and explainable methodology for engineering GenAI-enabled tools for enterprise-scale monolithic modernization.
