AB  - As cities rapidly expand as global centers of production, consumption, and pollution, understanding and managing their metabolic systems has a core challenge in sustainable urban governance. This paper systematically reviews two fundamental theoretical frameworks in urban metabolism research: (1) ecological thermodynamics, which reveals the dependence and constraints of energy and matter between urban systems and surrounding environment, and (2) urban scaling theory (complemented by planetary boundary concepts), which reflects the health status and safe operating space of cities. Based on these theories, we construct a knowledge graph of urban metabolism from a living-system perspective, encompassing subsystems such as the basic frameworks of urban metabolism, metabolic flow, disturbance-response mechanisms, and adaptive feedback. To translate theory into practice, we propose a closed-loop governance paradigm structured around “diagnosis-intervention-feedback” and “evolution-adaptation-regulation” patterns. Despite these theoretical advances, significant bottlenecks remain in data integrity, capturing complex system dynamics and future predictive ability. Artificial intelligence (AI) offers a critical pathway to overcome these challenges. Emerging AI technologies, including large language models and advanced neural networks, have demonstrated significant potential in data integration and calibration, causal mechanism recognition, generalizable modeling, and regulatory optimization. Building on this, integrating AI with traditional metabolic approaches can uncover complex urban patterns and mechanisms, providing actionable insights for sustainable urban development.
