AB  - Constructing knowledge graphs (KGs) from unstructured industrial operations and maintenance (O&M) records requires resolving ambiguous terminology, redundant expressions, and fragmented diagnostic knowledge. Large language models (LLMs) can support this process, but automated construction remains vulnerable to errors that require domain expertise to identify. We propose HitL-MKF, an LLM-driven framework for human-in-the-loop multi-level knowledge fusion. The framework first extracts entities and relations from maintenance records using task-specific prompts. It then applies entity alignment and path fusion, with HitL-Vote routing uncertain fusion decisions to experts. The resulting fault logic chains provide the knowledge used by Two-Stage GraphRAG to construct query-specific reasoning paths and generate diagnostic suggestions. We constructed the KG from 1,421 maintenance work orders and evaluated diagnostic responses on 209 held-out questions from a single computer numerical control (CNC) manufacturer. The complete framework achieved a BERT-F1 of 0.6493 and a Judge Score of 8.1109 ± 0.0668, alongside improved graph connectivity. The framework provides inspectable reasoning paths to support locally deployed, offline interactive diagnosis and maintenance decisions.
