AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) and other foundation models are increasingly trained and fine-tuned using data distributed across organizations and edge devices. Federated Learning (FL) is a natural paradigm for such collaboration, yet privacy leakage from model updates remains a major barrier, especially because powerful generative priors can amplify reconstruction and inference attacks in the LLM era. Privacy-Preserving Federated Learning (PPFL) based on multi-key homomorphic encryption, notably the CDKS scheme proposed by Chen et al. at CCS 2019, enables server-side aggregation over encrypted updates without sacrificing model utility. However, CDKS-based PPFL requires each participant to disclose a partial decryption value, and naive disclosure allows an honest-but-curious server (or colluding participants) to recover individual plaintext updates. In this paper, we propose CDKS-LSS, a PPFL framework that integrates Shamir-style Linear Secret Sharing (LSS) into the CDKS decryption workflow. Our design (i) removes the need for a trusted third party, (ii) upgrades a critical part of the decryption-stage confidentiality from computational security to information-theoretic security via LSS over Rq, and (iii) supports latency-tolerant aggregation by allowing the server to complete decryption after receiving only a threshold t of shares rather than all N participants. We formalize security under a semi-honest and collusion model and analyze communication costs. The resulting protocol provides a practical, post-quantum-secure cryptographic primitive for privacy-preserving federated training and fine-tuning of LLMs and other high-capacity models in cross-silo and cross-device settings.
