AB  - You’ve mastered the basics of KServe: setting up models, understanding its architecture, and deploying a simple predictor. But that’s just scratching the surface. Real-world machine learning systems rarely stop at “hello world.” They demand flexibility, interpretability, and the ability to evolve. That’s where the real engineering begins. In this chapter, we’re going to step into that next level: custom transformers, explainers, generative inference, and inference graphs–the tools that make KServe far more than just another model-serving framework. If you’ve ever deployed a model and later realized you needed to preprocess data differently, add postprocessing logic, or explain why a prediction was made, you already know the pain of patching together ad-hoc scripts and redeploying everything. I’ve been there too, hacking Flask routes for data transformations and running explainability code in a separate service that constantly drifted out of sync with the main model. It worked, technically, but it wasn’t scalable, repeatable, or fun. KServe changes that story. It provides first-class abstractions for these challenges: transformers to handle input/output logic, explainers to surface model insights, and inference graphs to orchestrate complex workflows. Whether you’re serving a simple regression model or chaining multiple generative models together, KServe brings a clean, consistent way to define, extend, and deploy your inference logic. We’ll also take a deep dive into model observability and monitoring because, no matter how solid your model looks in testing, production has its own rules. You’ll learn how to track performance metrics, visualize latency and throughput, and set up logging and tracing to catch issues before they impact users. Then we’ll explore how KServe powers generative inferencing, serving large language models (LLMs) efficiently and at scale. Finally, we’ll venture into advanced inference graphs, which enable connecting multiple models, transformers, and explainers into unified, dynamic inference pipelines. We will cover transformers and explainers in depth; however, given the limited scope of this chapter and the overall book, readers are encouraged to refer to the KServe documentation for detailed information on generative inferencing and inference graphs, while we focus on the foundational concepts here. Readers can find all the examples used in this chapter under the `kserve-example’ folder attached with the source code of this chapter.
