KW  - Unit test generation
KW  - Software testing and debugging
KW  - Large language models
AB  - Context:
Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in automated unit test generation in recent years. However, existing methods tend to exhibit deficiencies in capturing dependency semantics, managing context interference, and identifying semantic associations between library code and key methods. These limitations hinder the effectiveness of automated test generation, particularly for methods with high dependency complexity.
Objective:
This paper addresses the aforementioned challenges by proposing a multi-agent collaboration method based on task decomposition for automated unit test generation. The approach decomposes the complex unit test generation task into three collaborative subtasks: focal method information collection, test task planning, and test case generation.
Methods:
Three specialized agents were designed to handle different aspects of the test generation process: a global dependency construction agent, an abstract test requirement transformation agent, and an engineering test code generation agent. This task decomposition strategy narrows the analysis scope at each stage, enabling each agent to focus on specific problem domains. Additionally, a coverage feedback mechanism was introduced to achieve continuous optimization of test generation quality through analysis of test execution results and incremental information supplementation.
Results:
Experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed method achieves an average line coverage of 84.4% and branch coverage of 79.2% for methods with high dependency complexity. These results show clear improvements over both existing large-model-based methods and traditional automatic test case generation approaches. The performance improvements are particularly notable in handling complex dependency relationships.
Conclusion:
This study introduces an automated multi-agent collaboration framework that aims to address the challenges of dependency semantics capture and context management in unit test generation. The in-depth analysis of dependency handling capabilities in focal methods suggests the potential reliability and generality of the proposed approach, indicating its promise for practical application in software testing scenarios.
ER  - 

