AB  - Behavioral authentication in multi-agent LLM systems requires distinguishing identity binding from behavioral consistency. We provide an edge-oriented benchmark of 10,000 validated GPT-4.1 conversations and 19,216 windows across eight workflows, four roles, and seven conditions. TF-IDF reaches 0.8313 overall and 0.8571 validation-selected same-role macro-F1, but accepts 80.5% of wrong-claim retargets. Train-only per-agent enrollment lowers pooled wrong-claim acceptance to 5.77% with 95.92% genuine acceptance, while its original benchmark macro-F1 falls to 0.4137. These results separate profile matching from compromise screening rather than establish robust identity authentication. A combined Ed25519, freshness, and behavioral prototype blocks the tested signature and request-replay failures, but not valid-key compromise or freshly re-signed old content. On Raspberry Pi 5, its median endpoint latency is 8.03 ms versus 7.75 ms for behavior alone. Optimized INT8 MobileBERT reduces board-input energy by 45.27% relative to ONNX FP32. Transfer remains style-dependent, and a counterbalanced framework-native pilot does not establish ranking transfer. At matched 1% validation FAR, quality-filtered history-conditioned rewrite success is below 1%, but these operating points reject 37%–43% of genuine same-role windows; independent step-up verification would be required. The artifact supports reproducible evaluation of these security and efficiency trade-offs.
