KW  - LLM evaluation
KW  - Cybersecurity benchmarks
KW  - Benchmark saturation
KW  - Multiple-choice questions
KW  - Empirical measurement
KW  - Model comparison
AB  - Cybersecurity benchmarks that rely on multiple-choice questions (MCQ) were designed to measure and rank large language model capabilities. But what happens when the models outpace the measurement instrument? This paper provides reproducible, live-API evidence that easy-to-medium difficulty cybersecurity MCQs—the kind that form the bulk of most existing benchmark question banks—have reached saturation for current-generation models on this question bank. We evaluate eight frontier and near-frontier large language models across 250 cybersecurity questions spanning seven security dimensions, with correct-answer positions balanced across the bank so that fixed-position guessing cannot inflate scores. One model achieves a perfect score and all eight score at or above 97.6%; the top-to-bottom spread is 2.4 percentage points. The bank remains concentrated in difficulty tiers 1–3 (200 of 250 items), so the saturation finding is scoped to the easier end of the difficulty spectrum. On the expanded hard tail (50 items at tiers 4–5), scores span 90.0%–100%, showing that discrimination reappears where the questions are hardest. Paired item-level McNemar tests find no statistically significant differences among the eight models after correction for multiple comparisons. A response-level validation of answer extraction shows how easily measurement artifacts masquerade as capability gaps: a strict first-letter parser had under-scored two reasoning-mode models by 8.8 and 25.6 points in an earlier protocol run, and a re-measurement with complete response capture—with every parser disagreement manually validated—moves all eight models to 97.6%–100%. The residual failures are 21 generation-budget exhaustions (1.05% of calls), where reasoning-mode models consumed the entire output budget on internal reasoning and emitted no answer, plus a single committed wrong letter. We further show that a 39.2-point gap between scores predicted by a deterministic simulation calibrated on prior-generation benchmark trends and the live score of a current reference model (41.2 points below the observed 100% ceiling) collapses to 4.4 points when the same simulation is recalibrated on current data—consistent with the gap reflecting calibration staleness rather than a structural simulation error. Based on these findings, we argue that: (1) easy-to-medium cybersecurity MCQ benchmarks should be explicitly reframed as floor tests—they can flag underperforming models but cannot rank competent ones; (2) benchmark saturation should be treated as a publishable research output that tracks the movement of the evaluation frontier; and (3) the hard-question tail must be continuously replenished if MCQ discrimination is to survive at the frontier. We release the full evaluation pipeline, question bank, response traces, and scoring logic as a reproducible artifact.
