TY  - JOUR
T1  - Can federated fine-tuning improve LLM-based code summarization? An empirical investigation on open-source repositories
AU  - Kumar, Jahnavi
AU  - Tanguturi, Mokshith Reddy
AU  - Janapati, Lakshmana Sasaank
AU  - Chimalakonda, Sridhar
JO  - Journal of Systems and Software
VL  - 241
SP  - 113010
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/11/01/
SN  - 0164-1212
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2026.113010
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0164121226002438
KW  - Code summarization
KW  - Federated learning
KW  - Large Language Model (LLM)
KW  - Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)
AB  - Context:
Code summarization is essential for helping developers navigate complex projects. Recent advancements utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs) reveal limitations in the applicability of vanilla LLMs for proprietary code, motivating further fine-tuning on private datasets. Additionally, the siloed nature of code within institutions makes centralized fine-tuning impractical.
Objective:
This paper aims to investigate code summarization via LLMs by employing collaborative fine-tuning methods that maintain the privacy of proprietary code through Federated Learning (FL).
Experiments:
We build and evaluate FedLLM (LLM fine-tuned by FL) framework for code summarization across Python and Java programming languages. We utilize both LoRA and quantized LoRA (QLoRA) for parameter-efficient fine-tuning on generic LLaMA2-7B model as well as specialized CodeLLaMA model. Additionally, we explore different implementations of the FedAvg aggregation technique, including a novel matrix decomposition-based method termed FedDecomp. This experimental setup enables a comprehensive analysis of federated learning approaches and fine-tuning methods across programming paradigms.
Results:
Code summarization performance improves with CodeLLaMA. Both LoRA and QLoRA outperform non-federated models, with their relative performance varying by metric and setup. Our FedAvg implementation, FedDecomp, using matrix decomposition, surpasses the FedIT method. Fine-tuning with FL increases ROUGE scores from 7% to 24% for Python and from 6% to 27% for Java. The performance of Python FedLLM is comparable to or better than centralized training across all experiments. Java also exhibits similar behavior with CodeLLaMA.
Conclusion:
The collaborative approach to code summarization through FedLLM was examined in this study. The findings suggest that such an approach may provide potential benefits for clients operating under constraints such as data silos across organizations, privacy concerns related to sharing proprietary code, and limited training data available to individual participants. The results indicate that outcomes can vary depending on the specific federated aggregation strategies employed and the choice of underlying models. These observations underscore the importance of clearly detailing aggregation techniques, as different implementations may lead to substantially different performance characteristics. Future research may explore additional programming languages and broader software engineering applications. Furthermore, investigating alternative code representations, expanded baseline models, and diverse aggregation methods may offer further insight into the robustness and generalizability of federated approaches for LLM-based code tasks.
