KW  - Cost-sensitive learning
KW  - Green AI
AB  - Context: Software defect prediction research faces a fundamental ground truth crisis: manual validation achieves high precision but prohibitive costs, while automated heuristics enable scale at the expense of label quality. Recent Large Language Model (LLM) approaches introduce non-deterministic behavior and reproducibility challenges. Objective: This paper evaluates how a hybrid of static code metrics and process-based (evolutionary) metrics, combined with cost-sensitive learning, performs when trained on automatically generated labels whose noise has been quantified, and how far the results depend on the evaluation design. Method: We introduce JavaMLBugDetective, a deterministic framework aligned with Green AI principles. It labels file revisions with an enhanced SZZ implementation whose precision we measure with a manual audit of 398 candidates, 100 of them labeled independently by a second rater (49.5% precision, 95% CI 44.6–54.4%; Cohen’s κ=0.799), extracts 14 static and process features, and trains a cost-sensitive Random Forest. We evaluate it on 91,633 file revisions from three enterprise-scale Java projects (Apache Kafka, Google Gson, Apache Commons-IO; 25,480 commits, 17 years) under three evaluation designs: within-project 10-fold cross-validation, time-ordered evaluation (training only on earlier revisions), and cross-project evaluation. Results: Measured against the SZZ labels and under cross-validation, the hybrid model reaches a Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC) of 0.235 (Kafka), 0.382 (Gson), and 0.495 (Commons-IO), against 0 for trivial baselines, and process features outperform static features in every project. Time-ordered evaluation lowers ROC-AUC in every project (hybrid model: by 0.17–0.18 under rolling-origin evaluation and 0.12–0.18 under a chronological holdout). The hybrid model still ranks best by ROC-AUC, while the process-over-static margin shrinks from 0.07–0.21 to 0.01–0.04 under rolling-origin evaluation and 0.03–0.08 under a chronological holdout; in Kafka, the share of buggy labels also falls steeply over time, which drives threshold-based MCC towards zero. With raw features, static features do not transfer across projects (ROC-AUC  ≤  0.53), whereas process and hybrid features transfer weakly in five of six project pairs. Adding 20% symmetric label noise costs more MCC than the entire static-to-hybrid gain; under structured, SZZ-like noise, the ranking of the three feature sets is preserved up to 40% injected noise, apart from one tie. Conclusion: By ROC-AUC, combining static and process features ranked best under within-project cross-validation, both time-ordered designs, and five of six cross-project pairs. Much of the advantage of process over static features measured by random cross-validation, in particular through file age, does not carry over to forward-in-time prediction; we therefore recommend time-ordered evaluation for history-based features. Feature engineering does not compensate for further loss of label quality. The framework, datasets, and all experiment outputs are publicly available. All figures measure agreement with SZZ labels, not with verified defects.
