AB  - Current research in Chinese Text Correction (CTC) predominantly focuses on Chinese Spelling Check (CSC) and Chinese Grammatical Error Correction (CGEC), with efforts to harness the capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) for these tasks. However, the potential of LLMs for Chinese Semantic Error Correction (CSEC) remains underexplored, despite its importance and complexity. Furthermore, addressing semantic errors is crucial, as they are prevalent and can lead to syntactic irregularities or even introduce comprehension biases. To tackle these challenges, we explore the performance of LLMs in CSEC via instruction tuning. This work reveals both the potential and vulnerabilities of LLMs in CSEC, providing valuable insights for future research. Additionally, we introduce a plug-and-play example selection strategy tailored for CSEC, capturing fine-grained information across multi-dimensions to enhance the quality of selected examples. A re-scoring mechanism is designed to determine optimal correction from numerous candidates, yielding improved performance for LLMs. Experiments on the CSED-C and NaSGEC-Exam datasets show that our methods yield remarkable improvement of 12.8% and 1.17% in F0.5 score for ChatGLM3-6B-base model compared with using only instruction tuning, and further improvement is achieved when combined with the re-scoring mechanism. Overall, by offering practical solutions for CSEC with LLMs, our work contributes valuable insights and tools to the CTC field11Our code will be made publicly available following the acceptance of our paper..
