AB  - The escalating threat of synthetic media necessitates advanced detection frameworks that can analyze multimodal deception cues. This research presents a novel deepfake detection architecture that integrates spatiotemporal video analysis with semantic text verification through cognitively inspired fusion. Unlike conventional approaches requiring aligned data, our framework processes asynchronous video-text pairs using Video Vision Transformers (ViViT) for 3D patch-based temporal modeling, DeepSeek-LLM with causal attention for linguistic artifact detection, and Uncertainty-aware gating with reliability weighting (α-fusion) Key innovations include curriculum-guided training with hierarchical confidence sampling and neural evolutionary optimization. Evaluated on 9,299 samples of videos and other 9,299 samples of text from publicly available benchmarks, this innovative framework achieves an impressive state-of-the-art performance, boasting a remarkable accuracy of 0.9986, 0.9982 AUC and 99.9% specificity with a single misclassification, as well as 37% faster convergence than fusion baselines. The framework demonstrates robustness under modality degradation, achieving 98.3% accuracy at 40% corruption, and is deployed via ONNX-TVM compilation with a latency of 187 ms. This approach establishes new standards for multimodal deepfake detection while providing insights for cognitive computing architectures.
