AB  - Hateful memes on social networks often exploit non-linear interactions between images and text to convey implicit malicious ideologies, posing significant challenges to the robustness and interpretability of automated detection systems. Existing generative approaches frequently suffer from reasoning hallucinations, where models blindly align with the stances in user prompts rather than grounding their reasoning in visual facts. To address this, we propose a novel framework called co-attentive debater (Co-Debater), designed for hateful meme detection based on multimodal dialectical reasoning. First, we introduce a bidirectional adversarial fine-tuning strategy based on global counterfactual label flipping. Combined with a visually-anchored Chain-of-Thought mechanism, this strategy forces the model to generate dialectical arguments from opposing perspectives, thereby counteracting the model’s sycophancy bias. Second, we design a lightweight judge model embedded with a bi-directional co-attention mechanism. This architecture fuses the generated multi-perspective arguments with visual features to render the final verdict. Extensive experiments on three public benchmarks (Harm-C, Harm-P, and MAMI) demonstrate that Co-Debater outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving a Macro-F1 score of 92.11% on Harm-P. Qualitative evaluations further confirm that our framework reduces hallucinations, generating explanations that are logically rigorous and faithful to visual content.
ER  - 

