AB  - The advancement of performance-based earthquake engineering (PBEE) has highlighted the growing need for seismic resilience quantification and intelligent post-earthquake recovery assessment strategies. Among building components, unanchored non-structural components (NSCs), particularly indoor contents, constitute a significant portion of public facility investments. These components are diverse and highly susceptible to seismic damage, often leading to substantial system-level functional disruptions. There remains a lack of quantitative studies that link seismic damage to indoor NSCs with time-dependent resilience metrics associated with the functional recovery process. To address this gap, this study proposes a novel vision-based framework for assessing the seismic resilience of unanchored NSC systems. The framework comprises three core modules: vision-based damage recognition, LLM-assisted repair estimation, and seismic resilience quantification. The proposed framework leverages indoor surveillance videos to enable automated, data-driven evaluation of component-level damage and systemic functional performance. Visual evidence of interior changes and NSC damage, derived from surveillance videos, is fused to infer residual functionality and model the recovery process. Framework validation is achieved via shaking table experiments on a four-story scaled frame. The effectiveness of the framework in evaluating the seismic resilience of indoor unanchored NSC systems is further demonstrated by full-scale test results, providing real-world evidence of its generalizability.
ER  - 

