AB  - Automated security validation in enterprise networks requires agents that can reason over large combinatorial action spaces without falling into repetitive exploit loops. We present a hybrid architecture that combines Qwen-1.5B as a probabilistic action-prior generator with Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) as a simulation-based verification layer. Before executing any action, the agent runs 100 internal MCTS rollouts guided by a PUCT selection rule; failed actions are suppressed via dynamic action masking, forcing the search toward alternative attack vectors. Evaluation on the Chain-10 scenario in Microsoft CyberBattleSim shows that the hybrid agent achieves Local Privilege Escalation (LPE) in the episodes where valid exploit chains exist, with an average Steps to First Discovery of 1.67, substantially fewer steps than a standalone LLM baseline that exhibits persistent hallucination loops. The aggregate success rate across all episodes is 6.67%; therefore, we interpret the result as a controlled proof-of-concept of successful exploit-chain verification rather than as a statistically generalizable performance estimate. These results support the principle that combining language-model intuition with deterministic search verification can improve stability over standalone LLM action selection in a controlled cyber-simulation setting.
