KW  - Attack inference
AB  - Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) is a complex and long-term form of attack targeting specific objectives. With the continuous development of technology, the techniques and strategies of attackers are evolving, which poses greater challenges to security defenders. Data provenance based on system audit data is an important method in current APT attack detection. However, previous works have exhibited several shortcomings: (1) requiring a large amount of high-quality training data to work effectively, (2) failing to adapt to the continuous evolution of system behaviors and attack methods in real-world environments, and (3) lacking in-depth understanding, analysis, and reasoning of stealthy and long-term attacks. In this paper, we introduce ApexSentinel, an adaptive, precise, and explainable APT detection approach based on Large Language Models (LLMs). ApexSentinel combines neural network anomaly detection, graph-based analysis, and the contextual analysis capabilities of LLMs. It leverages the implicit knowledge of LLMs to achieve fine-grained APT detection and localization and reconstruct attack footprints. Using state-of-the-art benchmark datasets, we demonstrate that ApexSentinel enables adaptive, precise, and explainable APT detection.
